; hark: root-hints = 127.0.10.1

SCENARIO_BEGIN parent-zone NS records in NODATA authority do not trigger a referral
;
; Regression for the Hickory-class bug (hickory-dns#3477). When an
; authoritative server returns a NODATA response (NOERROR rcode, empty
; ANSWER) for a record type it doesn't have, including NS records for the
; zone (or a *parent* zone) in the AUTHORITY section is RFC 2308 §2.2 legal.
; A broken resolver would treat those NS records as a delegation and either
; loop, re-query against itself, or narrow the bailiwick wrongly. Correct
; behavior: recognise the NS as same-zone-or-above, treat the response as
; NODATA, return NOERROR with empty answer.
;
; To actually fire the buggy path this scenario:
;   - asks for a type the auth has no record of (AAAA on a name with only A)
;   - has the auth return NODATA with same-zone NS in AUTHORITY
;   - has the auth ALSO leak parent-zone NS (which would point a buggy
;     resolver at a wholly different server, making the bug observable)
; If hark wrongly followed the leaked NS it would re-query the parent auth
; for `missing.example.com. AAAA` and produce a different query log; if it
; wrongly followed the same-zone NS it would loop. Either failure mode
; surfaces as either a wrong rcode or a timeout.

; ── Fake root server ───────────────────────────────────────────────
RANGE_BEGIN 0 100
  ADDRESS 127.0.10.1
  ENTRY_BEGIN
    MATCH opcode qname
    ADJUST copy_id copy_query
    REPLY QR NOERROR
    SECTION QUESTION
      com. IN A
    SECTION AUTHORITY
      com. 86400 IN NS a.gtld.fake.
    SECTION ADDITIONAL
      a.gtld.fake. 86400 IN A 127.0.10.2
  ENTRY_END
RANGE_END

; ── Fake .com authority ────────────────────────────────────────────
RANGE_BEGIN 0 100
  ADDRESS 127.0.10.2
  ENTRY_BEGIN
    MATCH opcode qname
    ADJUST copy_id copy_query
    REPLY QR NOERROR
    SECTION QUESTION
      example.com. IN A
    SECTION AUTHORITY
      example.com. 86400 IN NS ns.example.com.
    SECTION ADDITIONAL
      ns.example.com. 86400 IN A 127.0.10.3
  ENTRY_END
RANGE_END

; ── Fake example.com authority ─────────────────────────────────────
RANGE_BEGIN 0 100
  ADDRESS 127.0.10.3
  ; AAAA query for a name with no AAAA: NOERROR + empty ANSWER (NODATA).
  ; AUTHORITY section leaks both same-zone (example.com.) and parent-zone
  ; (com.) NS records. A correct resolver ignores both: same-zone NS is
  ; not a child delegation; parent-zone NS is above the current bailiwick.
  ENTRY_BEGIN
    MATCH opcode qname qtype
    ADJUST copy_id copy_query
    REPLY QR AA NOERROR
    SECTION QUESTION
      missing.example.com. IN AAAA
    SECTION AUTHORITY
      example.com. 3600 IN SOA ns.example.com. hostmaster.example.com. 1 7200 3600 86400 300
      example.com. 3600 IN NS ns.example.com.
      com. 3600 IN NS a.gtld.fake.
    SECTION ADDITIONAL
      ns.example.com. 3600 IN A 127.0.10.3
      a.gtld.fake. 3600 IN A 127.0.10.2
  ENTRY_END
RANGE_END

STEP 1 QUERY
ENTRY_BEGIN
  REPLY RD
  SECTION QUESTION
    missing.example.com. IN AAAA
ENTRY_END

STEP 2 CHECK_ANSWER
ENTRY_BEGIN
  MATCH rcode question
  REPLY QR RD RA NOERROR
  SECTION QUESTION
    missing.example.com. IN AAAA
ENTRY_END

; Trip conditions: a Hickory-class bug here would manifest as either
;   (a) wrong rcode — e.g. NXDOMAIN if hark wrongly believed the leaked
;       parent NS made `missing.example.com.` its own zone with no records
;   (b) SERVFAIL from a referral loop (`MaxReferralsExceeded`) if hark
;       kept re-following the same-zone NS records
;   (c) test timeout if hark hangs chasing its tail
; CHECK_ANSWER above catches (a) and (b); the harness's 5s UDP timeout
; catches (c). A more discriminating assertion (e.g. "hark hit exactly
; one auth for the final AAAA") requires the CHECK_QUERY_LOG format
; extension tracked in test-suite-plan-2026-05-10.md.

SCENARIO_END
